Code: Fast-BVH, an optimized Bounding Volume Hierarchy
Author: Brandon Pelfrey (brandonpelfrey@gmail.com)
        Borrowed AABB Intersection code referenced in BBox.cpp
Date: April 17, 2012

This code, which I am releasing for public use, provides an optimized Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH).
The vector and axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) code included requires SSE in order to function.
The entire build and intersection test functionality is recursion-less and is much more optimized than
the one found in say, PBRT (Physically Based Rendering, by Pharr + Humphreys). With that said, if you
see another cool trick to speed things up, I'd love to see it!

The BVH itself makes minimal assumptions on the items it may contain: Objects need only implement the 
getIntersection(), getBBox(), and getCentroid() functions in order to be queryable.
The included example code renders a million spheres distributed inside of a cube using the BVH (in <3 seconds on my old MacBook!)   

I have attempted to comment this code well and make everything well-understood. If there is an issue
with the code, a question as to how something works, or if you use this in a project, please let me know!

Quantized offline ray preview
=============================

A built BVH can be exported into a memory-efficient quantized tree for
offline ray previews (include/FastBVH/QuantizedBVH.h and
include/FastBVH/QuantizedTraverser.h, both pulled in by FastBVH.h).
DefaultBuilder, BVH and the original Traverser are used exactly as before:

    FastBVH::DefaultBuilder<float> builder;
    auto bvh = builder(primitives, box_converter);
    FastBVH::QuantizedBVH<float, Sphere> qbvh(bvh);  // bvh may be destroyed now
    FastBVH::QuantizedTraverser<float, Sphere, SphereIntersector> trav(qbvh, isect);
    auto hit = trav.traverse(ray);                   // OnlyTestOcclusion also supported

Quantization grid
-----------------
Each QuantizedNode stores its bounding box as uint16_t min[3]/max[3] plus
the usual start/primitive_count/right_offset layout fields: 24 bytes per
node instead of the full-precision Node. On every axis, grid point q
corresponds to

    parent.min + (parent.max - parent.min) * q / 65535

The root node spans the whole grid (0..65535) over the separately stored
root bounds (getRootBounds()); every other node is encoded against the
actually-decoded box of its parent, never against the original floating
point parent box. Encoding rounds outwards, so a decoded box always covers
the closed source box of its node, and each end overshoots by at most
2/65535 of the parent axis span plus 32*epsilon*max(1, |parent ends|).
A zero-width parent axis keeps its coordinate (no division by zero).
decodeQuantizedBounds(parent, node) is public and performs the decoding;
queries decode boxes on the fly into a per-call working stack, so the tree
is never decompressed into a floating point cache.

Lifetime and ownership
----------------------
QuantizedBVH owns only the quantized node array and the root bounds. It
never keeps the original floating point nodes and never keeps a copied
bounding box array, so the source BVH may be destroyed right after the
export; closest-hit and occlusion queries keep working from the compressed
nodes alone. Primitives follow the old rules: they are borrowed, not
copied, so the caller-owned primitive array must outlive the QuantizedBVH,
and leaf slots refer to the exact same primitives as the source tree.

Running the checks
------------------
    bash task_support/run.sh target

compiles the new headers and tests/quantized_target.cpp, runs the
encoding/traversal/integration case groups (level-by-level decode
conservativeness and tightness, brute-force closest/occlusion comparisons,
source-tree independence and borrowed-primitive identity), writes a small
windowless quantized_preview.ppm render that is checked pixel by pixel
against brute force, prints the actual node byte counts and primitive
callback counts, and finally re-runs the 64-sphere baseline.
